          %p
            The condition
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ProjectionMorphism"} (1)
            clearly makes sense also if the categories are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            but not necessarily
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+adjunction"} Hopf adjunction
            relative to the cartesian monoidal structures.
          %p This terminology is most commonly used in the following situations:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                When
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
                are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image"} direct image
                functors along a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                in a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrine"} hyperdoctrine
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                is a category and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \colon S^{op} \to Cat
                is an
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+category"} indexed category
                such that each category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
                and each functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo =
                      %mi P
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* = P f
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"}> existential quantifier
                , also written
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo !
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is said to satisfy Frobenius reciprocity, or the
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong Frobenius condition
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi f
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f\dashv f^*
                does. If the categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
                are cartesian but not closed then it still makes sense to ask for Frobenius reciprocity in the second form above, and in that case its logical interpretation is that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi x
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists x . (\phi \wedge \psi)
                is equivalent to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi x
                      %mo .
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\exists x.\phi) \wedge \psi
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                is not free in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ψ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            %li
              %p
                When
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
                part of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2C1%29-topoi"} (n,1)-topoi
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                of it, if the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!\dashv f^*
                satisfies Frobenius reciprocity, then the geometric morphism is called
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+n-connected+%28n%2B1%2C1%29-topos"} locally (n-1)-connected
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=0
                so that we have a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
                satisfying Frobenius reciprocity makes it an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+map"} open map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
                so that we have 1-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topoi"}> topoi
                , then it is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+geometric+morphism"} locally connected
                (see also
                = succeed ")." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+geometric+morphism"} open geometric morphism
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+map"} proper map
          %h3#InWirthmuellerContexts In six operations yoga
          %p
            The projection formula plays a notable role in Grothendieck’s yoga of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_! \dashv f^\ast \dashv f_\ast)
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+categories"} closed monoidal categories
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wirthm%C3%BCller+context"} Wirthmüller context
            (
            %a{:href => "#May05"}> May 05
            ),
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
            is a strong
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+functor"} closed monoidal functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ∼
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi
                  \\;\colon\;
                  f_!
                  \\big(
                  f^\ast a \otimes c
                  \\big)
                  \\overset{\sim}{\longrightarrow}
                  a \otimes (f_! c)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+formula"} projection formula
            also holds in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+context"} Grothendieck context
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier-Grothendieck+context"} Verdier-Grothendieck context
            (
            %a{:href => "#May05"}> May 05
            ).
          %h3#InWeakFactorizationSystems In weak factorization systems
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} weak factorization system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℛ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{L},\mathcal{R})
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete category
            is said to satisfy the
            %strong Frobenius condition
            when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}
            is closed under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            (i.e.,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℛ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
          %p
            The property seems to have been named by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#VanDenBergGarner2012"} Van den Berg and Garner (2012)
            %a{:href => "#InCategoryTheory"} Frobenius reciprocity for adjoint functors
            by the following observation (
            %a{:href => "#ClementinoGiuliTholen1996"}> Clementino, Giuli &amp; Tholen 1996, Proposition 1.3
            ):
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system"} proper orthogonal factorization system
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{E},\mathcal{M})
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              satisfies the Frobenius condition if and only if for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Sub
                      %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Sub
                      %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub_{\mathcal{M}}(X) : f_! \dashv f^* : Sub_{\mathcal{M}}(Y)
              satisfies the Frobenius condition (where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Sub
                      %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub_{\mathcal{M}}(X)
              is the poset of subobjects of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
              is pullback along
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
              sends
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              to the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              part of the factorization of
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f m
          %p
            When the category is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              A weak factorization system
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℛ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{L},\mathcal{R})
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              satisfies the Frobenius condition if and only if for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : Y \to X
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℛ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : Z \to Y
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℛ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi f
                    %mi g
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_f g \to X
              is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℛ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+models+of+dependent+type+theory"} categorical models of dependent type theory
            where types are interpreted as maps in a right class (as in (
            %a{:href => "#VanDenBergGarner2012"}> Van den Berg &amp; Garner 2012
            )), the Frobenius condition can thus be used to interpret
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#KapulkinLumsdaine2021"} Kapulkin &amp; Lumsdaine 2021, Lemma 2.3.1
          %p
            In the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+categories"} Quillen model categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+model+category"} right properness
            (
            %a{:href => "#GambinoSattler2017"}> Gambino &amp; Sattler 2017
            ):
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+category"} Quillen model category
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
              are closed under pullback is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+model+category"} right proper
              if and only if its (trivial cofibration, fibration) weak factorization system satisfies the Frobenius condition.
          %p
            A model category which has cofibrations closed under pullback and a (trivial cofibration, fibration) wfs satisfying the Frobenius condition is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+model+category"} locally cartesian closed model category
            and thus presents a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#closed_monoidal_functors_and_the_projection_formula Closed monoidal functors and the projection formula
          %p
            The following result isolates the connection between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+functor"} closed functors
            and the projection formula. We begin with some context.
          %p
            Recall that a monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒴
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
            is
            %strong
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category#left_right_and_biclosed_monoidal_category"} left closed
            if each functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒴
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒴
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \otimes - \colon \mathcal{Y} \to \mathcal{Y}
            has a right adjoint
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒴
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒴
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a, -] \colon  \mathcal{Y} \to \mathcal{Y}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
          %p
            A functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            between left closed monoidal categories is
            %strong lax closed
            it if preserves the internal hom and the unit object up to a specified map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi F
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat{F}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  F[a,b] \to [F a,F b],
                  \\qquad
                  F_0 \colon I \to F(I)
                  \\,,
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
            in both variables and obeying some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+laws"} coherence laws
            listed at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+functor"} closed functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphisms"} natural isomorphisms
            we call the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+closed+functor"} strong closed
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal functor
            betweeen left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+categories"} closed monoidal categories
            is lax closed (for a sketch of the argument see
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+functor"} closed functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal functor
            may not be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+closed+functor"} strong closed
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! \dashv f^\ast
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjunction
              between left
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+categories"} closed monoidal categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi
                    \\;\colon\;
                    f^*[a,b] \to [f^*a, f^*b]
            %p
              correspond
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijectively
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+maps"} natural maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi
                    \\;\colon\;
                    f_!
                    \\big(
                    (f^\ast a) \otimes c
                    \\big)
                    \\longrightarrow
                    a \otimes (f_! c)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Furthermore,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is an isomorphism if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              is, in which case we say the
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+formula"} projection formula
              holds.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒴
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
              are left closed monoidal categories and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒴
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! \colon \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}
              is left adjoint to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi 𝒴
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒳
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* \colon \mathcal{Y} \to \mathcal{X}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon f^*[a,b] \to [f^*a, f^*b]
            %p
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒴
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in \mathcal{Y}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}(c, f^*[a,b]) \to \mathcal{X}(c, [f^*a, f^*b]),
            %p
              for arbitrary
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathcal{X}
              (now natural in all three variables). By hom-tensor adjointness and the fact that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
              is the left adjoint of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
              we can rewrite this as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒴
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}(f_! c, [a,b]) \to \mathcal{X}(f^*a \otimes c,f^*b).
            %p Using both these facts again we obtain
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒴
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}(a \otimes f_! c, b) \to \mathcal{X}(f_!(f^\ast a \otimes c), b)\,.
            %p
              By the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
              this gives the desired natural map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mi c
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi \colon\;
                    f_!(f^\ast a \otimes c) \longrightarrow a \otimes f_! c\,.
            %p
              By running through this calculation one can see that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is invertible then all the other natural maps listed above are too, including
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              we can run the argument backwards and get
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              is invertible then so is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          %p
            It follows that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            is strong closed, the projection formula holds. Also if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is strong monoidal and the projection formula holds,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            is strong closed.
          %h3#relation_to_frobenius_laws_in_frobenius_algebras Relation to Frobenius laws (in Frobenius algebras)
          %p
            The name “Frobenius” is sometimes used to refer to other conditions on adjunctions, known as “Frobenius laws”. The formal structure of the Frobenius law appears in the notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \colon 1 \to \otimes \Delta
            denotes the diagonal transformation on a cartesian bicategory (e.g.,
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Rel
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi δ
                      %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^\dagger
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mo †
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \delta^\dagger \stackrel{\phi}{\to} (1 \otimes \delta^\dagger)(\delta \otimes 1)
          %p mated to the coassociativity isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi δ
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1 \otimes \delta)\delta \to (\delta \otimes 1)\delta
          %p
            and the
            %strong Frobenius law
            here is the assumption that
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                the 2-cell
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                is an isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mo †
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mo †
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \delta^\dagger \stackrel{\phi'}{\to} (\delta^\dagger \otimes 1)(1 \otimes \delta),
          %p
            mated to the inverse coassociativity, is also an isomorphism. However, it may be shown that if one of the Frobenius laws holds, then so does the other; see the article
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory+of+relations"} bicategory of relations
          %p It is very easy to make a slip and call the Frobenius law “Frobenius reciprocity”, perhaps all the more because there are close connections between the two. One example occurs in the context of bicategories of relations, as follows.
          %p
            Given a locally posetal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+bicategory"} cartesian bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and any object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Semilat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_B(i-, c)\colon Map(B)^{op} \to Semilat
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: Map(B) \to B
            is the inclusion, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Semilat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Semilat
            is the 2-category of meet-semilattices. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in \hom(i b, c)
            is thought of as a relation from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast r
            is the pulling back
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi r
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi b
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi r
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast r \coloneqq (a \stackrel{f}{\to} b \stackrel{r}{\to} 1)
          %p
            along
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast-
            preserves finite local meets. Indeed, the pushforward or quantification along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: a \to 1
            to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mi q
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo †
                  %mi a
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi q
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f q \coloneqq (b \stackrel{f^\dagger}{\to} a \stackrel{q}{\to} 1)
          %p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f \dashv f^\ast
            because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\dagger
            is
            %em right
            adjoint to the map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast-
            is a right adjoint, it preserves local meets.
          %p Frobenius reciprocity in this context, ordinarily written as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mi q
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \wedge \exists_f q = \exists_f (f^\ast r \wedge q),
          %p
            can then be restated for the hyperdoctrine
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_B(i-, c)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi q
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo †
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \wedge q f^\dagger = (r f \wedge q)f^\dagger
          %p
            for any map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to b
            and predicates
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \in \hom(a, c)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in \hom(b, c)
          %p
            Meanwhile, recall that a
            %strong bicategory of relations
            is a (locally posetal) cartesian bicategory in which the Frobenius laws hold.
          #FLtoFR.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              Frobenius reciprocity holds in each hyperdoctrine
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_B(i-, c)
              associated with a bicategory of relations.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_sketch Proof (sketch)
            %p
              One first proves that a bicategory of relations is a compact closed bicategory in which each object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              is self-dual. The unit here is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ε
                          %mo †
                    %mi b
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi δ
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_b = (1 \stackrel{\varepsilon^\dagger}{\to} b \stackrel{\delta}{\to} b \otimes b)
            %p and the counit by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi δ
                          %mo †
                    %mi b
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi ε
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_b = (b \otimes b \stackrel{\delta^\dagger}{\to} b \stackrel{\varepsilon}{\to} 1).
            %p
              Using this duality, each relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo :
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r: b \to c
              has an opposite relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mi op
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r^{op} \colon c \to b
              given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mi b
                    %mi c
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi r
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mn 1
                    %mi c
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi c
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mi c
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi b
                    %mi b
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \stackrel{c \otimes \eta_b}{\to} c \otimes b \otimes b \stackrel{1 \otimes r \otimes 1}{\to} c \otimes c \otimes b \stackrel{\theta_c \otimes b}{\to} b.
            %p
              It may further be shown that in a bicategory of relations, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to b
              is a map, then its right adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\dagger
              equals the opposite
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{op}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi q
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mi op
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \wedge q f^{op} = (r f \wedge q)f^{op}
            %p
              but in fact this is just a special case of the more general modular law, which holds in a bicategory of relations as shown
              %a{:href => "http://rfcwalters.blogspot.com/2009/10/categorical-algebras-of-relations.html"} here
              in a blog post by Walters. The modular law in turn depends crucially upon the Frobenius laws.
          %p
            Thus, in this instance,
            = succeed "." do
              %em Frobenius reciprocity follows from the Frobenius laws
          #FRtoFL.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p In a locally posetal cartesian bicategory, the Frobenius laws follow from Frobenius reciprocity.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Again, Frobenius reciprocity in a (locally posetal) cartesian bicategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              means that for any map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to b
              and any two relations
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \in B(a, c)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in B(b, c)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi r
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo †
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi q
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo †
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (q \wedge r f)f^\dagger \leq q f^\dagger \wedge r
            %p
              is an equality. One (and therefore both) of the Frobenius laws will follow by taking the following choices for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ε
                      %mi x
                      %mo †
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mi r
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ε
                      %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ε
                      %mi x
                      %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = \delta_x, \qquad q = \varepsilon_{x}^{\dagger} \otimes 1_x, \qquad r = \varepsilon_x \otimes 1_x \otimes \varepsilon_{x}^{\dagger}
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_x: x \to x \otimes x
              is the diagonal map and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ε
                      %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon_x: x \to 1
              is the projection. The remainder of the proof is best exhibited by a string diagram calculation, which is given here:
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Frobenius-reciprocity.pdf", :title => "Frobenius reciprocity implies the Frobenius law in a cartesian bicategory"} Frobenius reciprocity implies the Frobenius law in a cartesian bicategory
          %h2#examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Generally, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
              any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+geometric+morphism"} etale geometric morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (f_! \dashv f^\ast \dashv f_\ast)
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathbf{H}_{/X}
                    \\to
                    \\mathbf{H}_{/Y}
            %p
              has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+functor"} cartesian closed functor
              and hence (see there) exhibits Frobenius reciprocity.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+condition"} Beck-Chevalley condition
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wirthm%C3%BCller+context"} Wirthmüller context
          %h2#References References
          %p
            Discussion in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
          %ul
            %li#Hristova19
              Katerina Hristova:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Frobenius Reciprocity for Topological Groups
              %strong 47
              5 (2019) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/00927872.2018.1529773"}> doi:10.1080/00927872.2018.1529773
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.00871"} arXiv:1801.00871
          %p
            The term ‘Frobenius reciprocity’, in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrines"} hyperdoctrines
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Equality+in+hyperdoctrines+and+comprehension+schema+as+an+adjoint+functor"} Equality in hyperdoctrines and comprehension schema as an adjoint functor
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/LawvereComprehension.pdf"} pdf
          %p Lawvere defines Frobenius reciprocity by either of the two equivalent conditions (see “Definition-Theorem” on p.6), and notes that “one of these kinds of identities is formally similar to, and reduces in particular to, the Frobenius reciprocity formula for permutation representations of groups” (p.1).
          %p Related discussion is in:
          %ul
            %li#Seely83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+A.+G.+Seely"}> Robert A. G. Seely
                , p. 511 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hyperdoctrines, Natural Deduction and the Beck Condition
                %strong 29
                (1983) 505-542 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/malq.19830291005"}> doi:10.1002/malq.19830291005
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.mcgill.ca/seely/ZML/ZML.PDF"} pdf
            %li{:id => "Pavlović96"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Du%C5%A1ko+Pavlovi%C4%87"}> Duško Pavlović
                , p. 164 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Maps II: Chasing Diagrams in Categorical Proof Theory
                %strong 4
                2 (1996) 159–194 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/jigpal/4.2.159"}> doi:10.1093/jigpal/4.2.159
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.isg.rhul.ac.uk/dusko/papers/1996-mapsII-IGPL.pdf"} pdf
          %p A textbook source is around lemma 1.5.8 in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
          %p
            General discussion in the context of projection formulas in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            (not necessarily cartesian) is in
          %ul
            %li#May05
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Halvard+Fausk"}> Halvard Fausk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Po+Hu"} Po Hu
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
              = succeed "," do
                %em Isomorphisms between left and right adjoints
              %strong 11
              4 (2003) 107-131 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/11/4/11-04abs.html"}> tac:11-04
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0573/FormalFeb16.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Manifestations of the Frobenius reciprocity formula,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#InCategoryTheory"} in the sense of category theory
          %ul
            %li
              Andrea Ferretti, Ubiquity of the push-pull formula, MO Question 18799, March 20, 2010.
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/18799/ubiquity-of-the-push-pull-formula"} (link)
          %p Further MO discussion includes
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/132272/wrong-way-frobenius-reciprocity-for-finite-groups-representations"} Wrong-way Frobenius reciprocity for finite groups representations
          %p Relating to the Frobenius condition for weak factorization systems:
          %ul
